{"name":"Electronic","hex":"#556d88","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"electronic","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or pertaining to an electron or electrons.","Operating on the physical behavior of electrons, especially in semiconductors."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A device which runs on electricity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/electronic"}],"entries":[{"title":"Electronics","blocks":[{"text":"Electronics is a scientific and engineering discipline that studies and applies the principles of physics to design, create, and operate devices that manipulate electrons and other electrically charged particles. It is a subfield of physics and electrical engineering which uses active devices such as transistors, diodes, and integrated circuits to control and amplify the flow of electric current and to convert it from one form to another, such as from alternating current (AC) to direct current (DC) or from analog signals to digital signals."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Arduino_ftdi_chip-1.jpg/330px-Arduino_ftdi_chip-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronics","wikidata":"Q11650"},{"title":"Electronics (magazine)","blocks":[{"text":"Electronics is a discontinued American trade journal that covers the radio industry and subsequent industries from 1930 to 1995. Its first issue is dated April 1930. The periodical was published with the title Electronics until 1984, when it was changed temporarily to ElectronicsWeek, but was then reverted to the original title Electronics in 1985. The ISSN for the corresponding periods are: ISSN 0013-5070 for the 1930–1984 issues, ISSN 0748-3252 for the 1984–1985 issues with title ElectronicsWeek, and ISSN 0883-4989 for the 1985–1995 issues. It was published by McGraw-Hill until 1988, when it was sold to the Dutch company VNU. VNU sold its American electronics magazines to Penton Publishing the next year."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electronics_(magazine)","wikidata":"Q1326155"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Audion_receiver.jpg/500px-Audion_receiver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Audion_receiver.jpg/500px-Audion_receiver.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of the earliest Audion radio receivers, constructed by De Forest in 1914 ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAudion_receiver.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Componentes.JPG/500px-Componentes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Componentes.JPG/1280px-Componentes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ea/Componentes.JPG/500px-Componentes.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Various electronic components","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AComponentes.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Logic-gates-circuits.png/500px-Logic-gates-circuits.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Logic-gates-circuits.png/1280px-Logic-gates-circuits.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/57/Logic-gates-circuits.png/500px-Logic-gates-circuits.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A selection of logic gates, used extensively in digital electronics","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALogic-g